<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ufa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ufa is the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashkortostan">Republic of Bashkortostan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, at the confluence of the Belaya and Ufa rivers west of the southern Urals. It was founded as a Russian fortress in 1574 and became a centre for governing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashkirs">Bashkirs</a> and the region's Muslims. In 1918 it hosted the State Conference that created the anti-Bolshevik Ufa Directory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ufa.jpg" alt="Ufa" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Svetlov Artem · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ufa_view_from_Belaya_river_2022-05.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ufa lies near the land boundary between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> and Northern Asia, at the confluence of the Belaya, called Agidel in Bashkir, and the Ufa rivers. It stands on low hills forming the Ufa Plateau, west of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ural-mountains">Ural Mountains</a>, and stretches 53.5 kilometres from north to south. The name comes from the river, but the origin of the river's name is disputed: the linguist Aleksandr Matveyev proposed an Iranian origin, from a word for water.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Some accounts place a medieval city on the site from about the 5th to the 16th century. A town called Pascherti, meaning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashkirs">Bashkort</a>, appears roughly on the Belaya on the Pizzigano brothers' map of 1367, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catalan-atlas">Catalan Atlas</a> of 1375 and Gerardus Mercator's map of 1554, and Henri Cordier connected it with Ufa. Ibn Khaldun listed Bashkort among the largest cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, and the historian Peter Rychkov wrote that a great city stood there before the Russians came.</p><p>By order of Ivan the Terrible a fortress was built on the site in 1574, first named Tura-Tau after its hill; that year is now the official date of the city's foundation, and it received town status in 1586. Ufa became the seat of a separate governorate in 1781, and in 1802 the centre of the whole Orenburg Governorate. The Belaya River Waterway of 1870 and the Samara-Zlatoust Railroad of 1890 tied it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-russia">European Russia</a> and encouraged light industry. After the Soviet retreat of 1941 many industrial enterprises from the west of the country were evacuated to Ufa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ufa is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashkortostan">Bashkortostan</a>, with over 1.1 million people, a majority of them ethnic Russians with substantial numbers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashkirs">Bashkirs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a>. Its industry rests on oil refining, chemicals and mechanical engineering, and the oil company Bashneft has its headquarters there. The city has several universities, among them Bashkir State University, and lies on a historic branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trans-siberian-railway">Trans-Siberian Railway</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ufa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
